    "passage": "Classical computing architectures rely on binary digits, or bits, which can exist exclusively in a definite state of either 0 or 1. In stark contrast, quantum computing exploits the bizarre principles of quantum mechanics, specifically superposition and entanglement. A quantum bit, or qubit, can exist simultaneously in a linear combination of both states until an observation forces a wave function collapse. Furthermore, quantum entanglement links multiple qubits such that the physical state of one instantaneously dictates the state of another, regardless of spatial separation. These quantum properties allow quantum processors to execute complex combinatorial calculations in seconds, tasks that would require classical supercomputers thousands of years to complete. However, sustaining stable quantum coherence requires maintaining physical qubits at fractions of a degree above absolute zero inside specialized cryogenic dilution refrigerators. Environmental thermal vibration or stray electromagnetic noise can precipitate decoherence, destroying delicate quantum information instantly."
