    "createdAt": "2026-09-11T12:00:00Z",
    "passage": "Anthropogenic carbon dioxide emissions do not solely accumulate in the troposphere; approximately thirty percent is absorbed by global ocean surfaces. When carbon dioxide dissolves in seawater, it undergoes a cascade of chemical reactions that form carbonic acid. This process releases free hydrogen ions, which subsequently bind to ambient carbonate ions to form bicarbonate. Consequently, the concentration of bioavailable carbonate ions decreases precipitously. This chemical depletion directly impairs calcifying marine organisms—such as scleractinian reef-building corals, pteropods, and mollusks—which require carbonate ions to synthesize their calcium carbonate skeletons. Experiments demonstrate that when seawater pH drops below critical saturation thresholds, existing coral skeletons begin to dissolve faster than corals can precipitate new mineral layers. Because coral reefs harbor over twenty-five percent of all documented marine biodiversity, ocean acidification threatens the structural integrity of global marine ecosystems and the coastal fisheries that sustain millions of humans."
